    "message": {
     "content": "### The Bottom Line\n\n**Do not accept this offer as-is, but do not walk away yet either.** \n\nThis is a classic \"opening bid\" from Google. Because you were headhunted, are happy at Meta, and have strong existing compensation, **you have maximum leverage**. Lateral moves from Meta IC6 to Google L6 carry onboarding friction and performance risk; accepting a package that represents essentially flat cash compensation and a minimal total comp bump does not justify that risk.\n\n---\n\n### Offer Breakdown & Reality Check\n\n| Component | Your Meta Package (IC6) | Google Initial Offer (L6) | Delta / Assessment |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **-$5,000 (Google is paying less base)** |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | +$11,000 |\n| **Annual Cash** | **$276,000** | **$282,000** | **+$6,000 (negligible +2% increase)** |\n| **Equity (Yr 1)** | ~$250,000 | $270,000 (or $356k if front-loaded) | Dependent on vesting schedule |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | N/A | $0 | **Standard low-ball omission** |\n| **Year 1 Total** | **~$526,000** | **~$552,000** (under 25% flat) | **Only ~5% increase overall** |\n\n#### Key Observations on Google's Numbers:\n1. **Base Salary is below your current pay:** Google's posted range is $183,000 \u2013 $271,000. Offering $235,000 puts you below the midpoint of the upper half and is a net reduction from your Meta base.\n2. **Missing Sign-on Bonus:** Google almost always has discretionary budget for a sign-on bonus ($50k\u2013$100k+ for L6 PMs) to bridge compensation gaps.\n3. **The Recruiter\u2019s Equity Narrative:**\n   * Google typically uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** (33% Year 1, 33% Year 2, 22% Year 3, 12% Year 4) for new hire grants. If your offer is 33/33/22/12, Year 1 equity is actually **$356,400**, making Year 1 TC ~$638,400.\n   * The recruiter\u2019s illustration assumed uniform 25%/yr vesting + stacked refreshers. Refresher amounts at Google are heavily performance-dependent and subject to shifting compensation curves; you should never evaluate an offer based on projected refreshers.\n\n---\n\n### What to Ask For (Your Counter-Proposal)\n\nTo make leaving Meta worth your while, target an offer that delivers **$620,000+ annualized value** with upfront protection:\n\n1. **Base Salary: Ask for $255,000 \u2013 $260,000**\n   * *Rationale:* You cannot take a base salary pay cut coming from Meta ($240,000), and their posted band goes up to $271,000.\n2. **Sign-On Bonus: Ask for $75,000 \u2013 $100,000**\n   * *Rationale:* Offsets the risk of switching, forfeiting near-term bonus/vesting cycles at Meta, and compensates for the initial cash parity.\n3. **Equity (GSUs): Ask for $1,250,000 \u2013 $1,300,000 over 4 years**\n   * *Rationale:* Brings annualized equity up from $270k to ~$315k\u2013$325k/year, aligning with upper-band L6 PM packages in the Bay Area.\n4. **Vesting Schedule Confirmation:** Explicitly ask the recruiter to confirm whether this grant follows the standard **33/33/22/12 schedule** or an equal 25% split.\n\n---\n\n### Suggested Negotiation Script\n\nYou can email or speak with the recruiter using this framework:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this offer together. I really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m excited about the scope of the role and the impact I could have at Google.*\n>\n> *As I mentioned early on, I wasn't actively looking to move because I'm in a great spot at Meta, currently tracking at ~$526k total compensation with a $240k base. Looking at the initial offer, the base salary is actually a step down from my current guaranteed cash, and without a sign-on component, the overall premium doesn't quite reflect the risk of walking away from my current trajectory and unvested equity.*\n> \n> *To make this an easy decision for me to sign, I would need to see the package reflect:*\n> - * **Base Salary:** $255,000 (to ensure a step forward from my current base)*\n> - * **Sign-on Bonus:** $80,000*\n> - * **Equity:** $1,250,000 GSU grant over 4 years*\n>\n> *If we can get the compensation to that level, I would be thrilled to sign immediately. Could you see if there is flexibility to adjust the package along these lines?\"*\n\n---\n\n### Decision Rule\n\n* **If they meet your counter (or get close, e.g., $250k base + $60k sign-on + $1.2M equity):** **Accept**, assuming you are energized by the Google team and product domain.\n* **If they refuse to move significantly (e.g., total Year 1 remains under $575k with no sign-on and lower base):** **Walk away gracefully.** Staying at Meta as a proven IC6 with momentum and predictable equity refreshes is financially superior to taking a lateral move on a mediocre opening bid.",
